1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,199
A hundred thousand manat sits in an equity fund whose annual log

2
00:00:04,299 --> 00:00:09,572
return is normal, with mean eight percent and standard deviation twenty percent.

3
00:00:09,672 --> 00:00:13,670
What is it worth, on average, in a year? The tempting answer,

4
00:00:13,770 --> 00:00:17,767
a hundred thousand times e to the point zero eight, is wrong.

5
00:00:19,866 --> 00:00:23,789
The fix is a moment-generating function, now for continuous variables.

6
00:00:24,883 --> 00:00:28,129
The log return is a symmetric bell. The fund's value is a

7
00:00:28,229 --> 00:00:31,827
hundred thousand times e to the R, and pushing the bell through

8
00:00:31,927 --> 00:00:35,232
the exponential makes it lopsided, with a long right tail.

9
00:00:35,316 --> 00:00:38,949
Take a good year and a bad year, one standard deviation either

10
00:00:39,049 --> 00:00:43,524
side. Against the middle value, the good year adds twenty-four thousand, the

11
00:00:43,624 --> 00:00:47,678
bad year takes away only twenty. Gains are stretched more than losses

12
00:00:47,778 --> 00:00:50,628
are shrunk, so the average sits above the middle.

13
00:00:50,716 --> 00:00:54,769
And the average of e to the R is the moment-generating function

14
00:00:54,869 --> 00:00:57,406
of R at t equal to one: e to the mu plus

15
00:00:57,506 --> 00:01:01,624
sigma squared over two. That gives about one hundred and ten and

16
00:01:01,724 --> 00:01:06,107
a half thousand. The hundred and eight thousand was only the median.

17
00:01:07,200 --> 00:01:11,202
How do you compute an MGF? For a gamma density, multiplying by e

18
00:01:11,302 --> 00:01:15,049
to the t y only weakens the decay. As t grows, the integrand

19
00:01:15,149 --> 00:01:19,664
swells and stretches, but it keeps the gamma shape, with a larger scale.

20
00:01:20,750 --> 00:01:25,775
And we already know the area under any gamma shape, so there's nothing

21
00:01:25,875 --> 00:01:30,241
to integrate: the MGF is one minus beta t, to the power minus

22
00:01:30,341 --> 00:01:35,513
alpha, for t below one over beta. Recognise the kernel; don't integrate.

23
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:39,483
Tchebysheff's theorem holds here too, and the proof

24
00:01:39,583 --> 00:01:42,233
is a picture. The variance averages the squared

25
00:01:42,333 --> 00:01:45,042
distance from the mean, weighted by the density.

26
00:01:46,133 --> 00:01:51,288
Throw two things away. First the middle, within k standard deviations.

27
00:01:51,388 --> 00:01:56,092
Second, in the tails, replace the squared distance by its floor,

28
00:01:56,192 --> 00:02:00,897
k squared sigma squared. Both steps only make the total smaller.

29
00:02:00,983 --> 00:02:04,288
What's left, k squared sigma squared times the tail

30
00:02:04,388 --> 00:02:08,495
probability, can't exceed sigma squared. Divide, and that's the

31
00:02:08,595 --> 00:02:11,567
theorem. What we threw away is why it's loose.

32
00:02:12,666 --> 00:02:15,580
How loose? On a log scale: the bound against

33
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:18,731
the true tails of an exponential and a normal.

34
00:02:18,816 --> 00:02:23,169
At three standard deviations the bound allows eleven percent; the exponential

35
00:02:23,269 --> 00:02:26,639
has under two, the normal a quarter of one. Assuming nothing

36
00:02:26,739 --> 00:02:29,531
about shape keeps it safe, and costs it precision.

37
00:02:30,616 --> 00:02:34,996
So: the moment-generating function is now an integral doing the same

38
00:02:35,096 --> 00:02:38,816
jobs. For a normal log return, it gives the average value,

39
00:02:38,916 --> 00:02:44,284
and volatility raises it. Recognise kernels instead of integrating. And Tchebysheff

40
00:02:44,384 --> 00:02:47,182
is safe for every shape, and loose for most.

